{"id": "1c692c26-2058-4efb-8659-171840a490c9", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The user wants me to look into what's causing the slowness, and they've already flagged EBS IO balance draining as a likely culprit. This seems like a fairly bounded investigation — verify the instance and volume metrics to confirm the burstable EBS bandwidth/IOPS limit is actually the bottleneck, rather than a wide-open root-cause hunt.

I'll outline the plan to the user, then delegate to a sub-agent to pull CloudWatch metrics for the relevant instance/volume over the last hour (18:00-19:04Z) against an earlier baseline, since that data is token-heavy to inspect directly.

"}]}

{"id": "4d06a4c4-ea8a-4b78-a1d4-95fec20c9ec2", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll check the instance's EBS and CPU credit metrics, along with the volume's performance, to confirm the IO balance drain and see what's driving it.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_I2HPlBWP0NdN8TDQC3odIO", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "ebs-io-balance-check", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["exploring-metrics"], "prompt": "{
  "task": "Verify and quantify whether the burstable EC2 instance is being throttled on EBS performance (EBS IO balance depletion), and describe what is driving the IO.",
  "context": "User reports the application on this instance became slow over the last ~30 minutes. They say the attached gp3 volume is provisioned for more IOPS/throughput than the instance type can deliver and that EBSIOBalance% is draining. Confirm or refute this with data. This is a focused, shallow pass: report findings and suggest deeper areas rather than going exhaustive.",
  "resources": ["EC2 instance i-0123456789abcdef0_1", "EBS volume vol-0123456789abcdef0", "Account 012345678901_1 unless the instance is found in another associated account", "Region us-east-1 unless found elsewhere"],
  "timeframe": "Incident window 2026-11-11T18:30:00Z to 2026-11-11T19:04:00Z; baseline window 2026-11-11T12:00:00Z to 2026-11-11T18:00:00Z",
  "criteria": "1) Instance type (describe-instances) and its documented baseline/max EBS IOPS, throughput and burst behaviour; volume type, provisioned IOPS and throughput (describe-volumes) and whether provisioned values exceed the instance EBS limits. 2) AWS/EC2 metrics EBSIOBalance% and EBSByteBalance% trend, plus EBSReadOps/EBSWriteOps/EBSReadBytes/EBSWriteBytes at the instance level; compare to instance limits. 3) AWS/EBS volume metrics: VolumeReadOps, VolumeWriteOps, VolumeReadBytes/WriteBytes, VolumeQueueLength, VolumeTotalReadTime/WriteTime, VolumeIdleTime, BurstBalance if present; derive average latency per op. 4) CPUUtilization and CPUCreditBalance / CPUSurplusCreditBalance / CPUSurplusCreditsCharged to rule in or out CPU credit exhaustion as a contributing or alternative cause. 5) Note when IO balance started dropping, when it hit or approaches 0, and any step change in workload (IO rate rise) at that time. Also note network metrics only if quickly available. Report concise numbers with timestamps and say what is confirmed vs unknown."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Check EC2 and EBS metrics for IO balance throttling"}], "created_at": null}

{"id": "78dcf752-a127-403d-9341-ec357e5af754", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The subagent came back empty-handed, and I don't have direct AWS access myself, so I should be upfront about that limitation rather than pretending otherwise. I could try a quick list_investigations call to see if there's related context, but it's not obviously useful—better to just report honestly and offer general guidance.

On the EBSIOBalance% issue itself: this points to burstable instance types where EBS performance runs on burst credits, and once that balance hits zero the volume gets throttled to baseline throughput/IOPS. If they've provisioned gp3 beyond what the instance size can actually push, that capacity is being wasted—resizing to a larger or non-burstable instance type, or reducing IO demand, would fix it. Changing the instance type would need a stop/start and require approval for the mutating action, so I shouldn't overpromise on fixing it directly—just lay out the diagnosis and options clearly and concisely.

"}]}

